The handoff is written and not committed. It's on the Windows VM at `C:\Users\guitest\Downloads\printer-agent-tools.md`, and the copy there matches the original byte for byte. It replaces the contents of [agent-docs/jusprin/printer-agent-tools.md](agent-docs/jusprin/printer-agent-tools.md) in the main checkout, so there's still one document. That file shows as modified; the committed version from `f4a3920443` stays in git history.

It covers the model–tool interaction only, in the sections we agreed:
- the starting point and what's wrong with it;
- the rule;
- a who-handles-what table for every tap and message;
- session setup for each mode;
- both tool contracts, with worked examples that double as acceptance tests;
- what the app tells the model after its own actions;
- what to remove, and back-end fixes;
- verification, your decisions, and open questions (none).

Checking the code while writing turned up three things now in the doc:
- **The model can't see what the app did.** The design relies on the app adding a line to the conversation after each of its own actions. Today those lines are left out of what the model receives, deliberately ("Notes stay out of the model's context", `AgentHost.cpp` around line 1608). The doc asks for a way to get them into the model's context, with tests.
- **The printer list needs names, not just ids.** The app's printer ids are often meaningless codes: `BBL/N1` is the Bambu Lab A1 mini, `BL-P001` the X1 Carbon. The list the model gets must include each printer's brand and model name, or it can't match what people type. The fix branch sends only the id and the size, so it probably has this bug. I found that by reading its code; I didn't test it.
- **Only two taps start a new model reply:** "Not this one", and a network printer the app can't match to a known model. Every other tap only adds a line to the conversation.

